On Dec 26, 10:44 pm, Ray Dillinger <b...@sonic.net> wrote:
> There are a number of people who are motivated to code
> roguelike games from scratch.  I'm one of them....
> Even if I were to code "Neohack", in which everything in
> nethack were to be preserved unless I thought there was
> a good reason not to, there'd still be a bunch of major
> differences from nethack.

I'm not sure what the intent of posting is, then. Is this a call for
more developers? Just a string of YANI? Something else?

> * (copyright issues)

I believe these are not a problem. After all, D&D has been doing it
for a long, long time. Tolkien's works may be under copyright, but
there is such a thing as "fair use". Or did you want to get rid of all
the gnomes, dwarves, elves, orcs and (tricksy) hobbits(es) while
you're at it?

> * (Magic markers)

I like the idea that failing to write a scroll or spellbook results in
a scroll/spellbook of graffiti. It can be blanked, but otherwise does
nothing useful. The scroll, if read, gives a random rumour (associated
with the object, not re-drawn each time!) but does not consume the
scroll; if dropped, it merges with the floor and the message is
written in the dust (perhaps burned in for blessed scrolls, written in
blood for cursed ones?) The spellbook, if read, appears to contain
obscenities (no effect, except perhaps an alignment adjustment;
perhaps a cursed one curses you like a cursed unicorn horn).

Oh, and there would be much lower chances of writing something
successfully, and perhaps a chance of failure for writing known things
as well. The level of spellbook / cost of scroll? would also be taken
into account in judging the difficulty of writing.

I don't think they need to be rarer, though. Quite the opposite, even,
with the other adjustments.

> * (pudding farming)

They *aren't* "generated with new equipment". The items are spawned at
the time of death, AFAIK.

My proposals:

1) splitting halves maximum as well as current HP, rounds down, and
does not occur if the attack deals zero damage.
2) Monsters generated by any create-monster effect, splitting by a
player weapon, or anything else that's consciously triggered by the
player (I can't think of anything?) get a 'usergen' flag, which
suppresses the death-drop code. Gremlins should not be affected by
this, though; it's their choice to split.

>
> * (wishing)

My set of proposals (they kind of depend on each other though):

1) /oW no longer exist. /oD become "cheaper", but cannot be generated
in a shop (or perhaps shopkeepers become magic-resistant). Magic lamps
become more common, but are still hard to polypile for. The castle
contains a guaranteed magic lamp named Aladdin's Lamp, in a locked
chest accompanied by random but guaranteed-valuable gems.
2) The magic lamp has charges, and is generated at (0:3). It cannot be
recharged in any way; attempts to use a scroll, fountain etc. simply
have no effect. #rub uses a charge, and generates a djinni as per the
current rules. (So a blessed lamp yields 2.4 wishes on average.)
3) AI is added to the wish-handling code, which takes the lamp's
status (affects the djinni's demeanour, above and beyond the check for
whether it's willing to grant a wish) into account in interpreting the
wish (see Rachel's post re "interpretation").

> * (artifact abilities)

I like this a lot, in theory. I don't think e.g. getting a "bonus"
resistance on FoBo is a real problem for game balance, either. I'll
warn though that this kind of randomization is the first step down the
path to Angband...

> * (AC blah blah blah)

I agree that the combat system needs a complete overhaul. I don't
think upgrading to the latest d20 system stuff will work that well,
either.

> * (Unicorn horns)

I don't see why they're a problem as is. If they cured hunger, I might
agree with you. If anything, I'd change how sickness works to make it
more challenging.

> * Item curses cast by liches et al would mostly end when
>   that particular lich gets destroyed.

I really really don't like this. It sounds like the ROI on the coding
effort would be pathetic, even if you agree with the motivation, which
I don't.

> * (other blessing stuff)

I still don't like it, except perhaps "restriction on number of
explicitly-blessed items being carried", although that still seems
likely to be a lot of effort for little gain.

> * (fooproofing)

My proposals:
1) Get rid of disenchanters (they hardly ever succeed in vanilla, and
in D&D I think they must have been created by incompetent DMs needing
a band-aid for giving out too much loot). Get rid of water traps (it
just doesn't make sense that dumping water on you can instantly rust
your equipment) too. Other sources of equipment damage stay.
2) Items have a small chance of being destroyed entirely when damaged.
The chance increases with increasing amounts of existing damage.
Otherwise there is no effect.

(If you take out fooproofing entirely, you effectively get rid of
water traps and rust monsters, anyway. I think rust monsters are a lot
more interesting conceptually than disenchanters.)

> * (artifact stuff)

I like most of what has already been said here, especially the bit
about "little rules" for each deity.

And now, two suggestions of my own:

1) Conduct changed to be selected a priori, and enforced. Foodless?
Corpses become unenchantable weapons, fortune cookies become tools,
and other food items disappear. Vegetarian/vegan? Subsets of that.
Atheist? You can't see the flashes from altars and #pray just gives a
message about feeling a moment of weakness. Weaponless? (w)ield just
brandishes the weapon threateningly. No polymorph? The wands and
potions disappear, and any polyself effect, if controlled, just makes
you a new (wo)man (this would become one conduct to replace polyself/
polyother, and you could technically still polyself, just not very
usefully).

2) Could we possibly make intelligent monsters not try to zap you with
the same wand after it's seen that it won't work (MR/reflection)? And
could we give powerful monsters some more interesting wands more
often? (Maybe nurses have a high chance to get a wand of probing?)
Frankly, the fact that wands seem to be generated much more frequently
in Gehennom monster inventories makes Gehennom much easier, when the
intent was presumably to make it harder.


